WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.957
The Data Pipelines module reads data from any source of your site, transforms

00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:10.376
it, and delivers it as files or content items, without writing code.

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:14.915
This video shows why it exists, how to build, preview, publish and run

00:00:14.915 --> 00:00:18.020
a pipeline, and how to automate and extend it.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.142
The data of your site lives in content items, users and saved queries,

00:00:27.142 --> 00:00:30.950
and you can define your own data sources to bring in more.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.639
Sooner or later, someone needs that data somewhere else:

00:00:34.639 --> 00:00:39.280
a spreadsheet for the team, a feed for a partner, or a file on a server.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:44.493
Each of those exports usually means custom code, a scheduled job, or a

00:00:44.493 --> 00:00:47.370
script that only one developer understands.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.942
A data pipeline describes the export once, in the admin:

00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:56.073
where the data comes from, how it changes, and where it goes.

00:00:56.073 --> 00:01:00.820
Anyone allowed can preview it, run it, and see what each run delivered.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.131
Data sources provide the data:

00:01:07.131 --> 00:01:11.059
data sets, such as the items of a content type, the users, or

00:01:11.059 --> 00:01:14.518
the results of a saved query, each with typed fields.

00:01:14.518 --> 00:01:19.502
The Data Pipelines Core feature shares them, with the formula functions and the

00:01:19.502 --> 00:01:23.430
data operations, with other modules, such as reporting tools.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.978
A pipeline connects steps through ports that carry rows or files.

00:01:28.978 --> 00:01:34.655
Sources read rows, transforms change them, file steps write them as CSV, Excel

00:01:34.655 --> 00:01:38.440
or JSON files, and destinations deliver the files.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:43.516
A run streams the rows through the steps in batches, in the background,

00:01:43.516 --> 00:01:46.942
with the access of the user who published the pipeline.

00:01:46.942 --> 00:01:50.650
A sort or a join keeps its memory within a limit, and writes

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.560
the rest to temporary files.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.476
One feature turns it all on.

00:01:55.476 --> 00:02:00.827
The steps of the media library, email, content items and workflows appear when

00:02:00.827 --> 00:02:02.929
those features are enabled.

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:08.980
The FTP and SFTP uploads are modules of their own, as they rely on third-party libraries.

00:02:13.171 --> 00:02:17.061
Data Pipelines is in the Data category of the Features page.

00:02:17.513 --> 00:02:22.138
Enable it, and its steps follow the features the site already uses.

00:02:22.138 --> 00:02:25.813
The FTP and SFTP uploads have features of their own.

00:02:30.001 --> 00:02:35.783
Under Tools, Data Pipelines, the Pipelines page lists every pipeline, with its published

00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:40.151
version, its unpublished changes, and the result of its last run.

00:02:40.602 --> 00:02:42.742
Search finds a pipeline by its name.

00:02:43.437 --> 00:02:45.362
Edit opens the designer.

00:02:45.362 --> 00:02:50.687
The Actions menu runs the pipeline now, opens its run history, or deletes it.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:52.468
Let's create one.

00:02:52.468 --> 00:02:56.057
Add Pipeline asks for its name, then opens the designer.

00:03:00.248 --> 00:03:04.021
The designer shows the steps on the left, the canvas in the middle,

00:03:04.021 --> 00:03:07.743
and the settings, issues and history of the pipeline on the right.

00:03:07.743 --> 00:03:09.708
A pipeline starts with a source.

00:03:10.158 --> 00:03:13.142
The step opens in the panel below the canvas.

00:03:13.142 --> 00:03:15.015
Pick the data set to read:

00:03:15.015 --> 00:03:18.058
here, the items of the Blog Post content type.

00:03:18.508 --> 00:03:22.668
Pick the fields to read, in their order, and add filters, such as

00:03:22.668 --> 00:03:25.128
the posts published since September.

00:03:27.064 --> 00:03:31.479
The Data tab previews the first rows, with the settings of the draft.

00:03:31.479 --> 00:03:33.834
A preview never delivers anything.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.903
Click the output of a step to add the next one.

00:03:40.903 --> 00:03:44.877
The menu offers only the steps that accept what the output carries.

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:50.072
Add calculated fields computes fields from formulas, with typed results.

00:03:50.072 --> 00:03:52.749
The headline is the title in capitals.

00:03:54.031 --> 00:03:58.134
The editor lists the fields and every function, with a search.

00:03:58.134 --> 00:04:01.694
Click a function, then a field, to write the formula:

00:04:01.694 --> 00:04:04.409
here, the month the post was published.

00:04:04.409 --> 00:04:08.331
Modules add their own functions with AddExpressionFunction.

00:04:08.783 --> 00:04:13.191
The Fields tab shows the fields a step receives and produces, with their

00:04:13.191 --> 00:04:16.413
types, which the designer infers from the formulas.

00:04:16.863 --> 00:04:25.453
Other transforms filter, select, sort, group and summarize, join, combine, deduplicate and limit rows.

00:04:29.638 --> 00:04:34.712
Create a file writes the rows as a CSV, Excel, JSON or JSON Lines file.

00:04:34.712 --> 00:04:37.084
Its options follow the format.

00:04:37.084 --> 00:04:42.818
Connect several steps to it, and each one gets its own worksheet in the workbook.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.930
The file name can use placeholders, such as the date of the run,

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.330
the name of the pipeline, or a parameter a workflow passes.

00:04:51.004 --> 00:04:54.398
Save to the media library stores the file in a folder.

00:04:54.398 --> 00:04:58.754
Its editor warns about the file types the media library doesn't accept.

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.963
A file can go to several destinations.

00:05:04.963 --> 00:05:09.445
Drag Share a download link from the library, and connect the file to it.

00:05:11.274 --> 00:05:15.247
A download link works only for its recipients, once they sign in, and

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:17.684
stops working after the days you choose.

00:05:18.134 --> 00:05:23.314
The Data tab of a destination shows the file it would receive, without delivering it.

00:05:27.516 --> 00:05:30.822
The draft saves as you work, and runs never use it.

00:05:30.822 --> 00:05:34.766
Publish creates a version, and runs use the published version.

00:05:35.218 --> 00:05:40.158
Versions lists the published versions, and restores any of them into the draft.

00:05:40.862 --> 00:05:43.270
Run queues a run in the background.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:48.732
The History tab follows it, and the canvas shows the rows and files each step produced.

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:54.002
Each run keeps its status, its steps, what it delivered, and a log.

00:05:58.189 --> 00:06:03.897
Run History, under Tools, Data Pipelines, lists the runs of every pipeline.

00:06:03.897 --> 00:06:07.139
Search by pipeline, or filter by status.

00:06:07.589 --> 00:06:10.111
A failed run shows why it failed:

00:06:10.111 --> 00:06:13.539
here, the API of a partner couldn't be reached.

00:06:13.989 --> 00:06:19.286
Shared Files lists the files shared through download links, with their recipients, their

00:06:19.286 --> 00:06:21.145
expiry and their downloads.

00:06:21.145 --> 00:06:23.399
A link can be revoked at any time.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.475
Recipients find the files shared with them on the Files shared with you

00:06:27.475 --> 00:06:30.160
page of the site, and download them once signed in.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.009
Sources read a data set of any data source, or the rows of

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:42.860
a CSV, Excel or JSON file of the media library.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:51.069
Transforms filter rows into matched and unmatched outputs, calculate, select and rename fields,

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:57.790
sort, group and summarize, join, combine, remove duplicates, and keep the first rows.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:04.453
File steps write CSV, Excel, JSON and JSON Lines files, and zip several

00:07:04.453 --> 00:07:06.680
files into one archive.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:14.325
Destinations deliver to the media library, download links, email, web APIs, FTP and

00:07:14.325 --> 00:07:17.270
SFTP servers, and content items.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:25.832
In a workflow, the Run Data Pipeline Task runs a published pipeline.

00:07:25.832 --> 00:07:31.020
It can wait for the run to end, and continue with the Succeeded or Failed outcome.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:36.508
It passes parameters, written with Liquid, and steps read them with the Parameter

00:07:36.508 --> 00:07:39.830
placeholder, such as the date a data source filters on.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:45.284
The Data Pipeline Run Completed Event starts a workflow when a run ends,

00:07:45.284 --> 00:07:48.240
such as to notify someone when a run fails.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.733
The DataPipelines section of appsettings.json sets the memory a step may use before

00:07:57.733 --> 00:08:02.134
it writes rows to temporary files, how many runs of a tenant execute

00:08:02.134 --> 00:08:06.000
at once, how long runs are kept, and the rows of a preview.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:10.503
The media library accepts Excel workbooks by default.

00:08:10.503 --> 00:08:14.766
To save CSV, JSON or zip files to it, add their extensions to

00:08:14.766 --> 00:08:16.930
its allowed file extensions.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.640
The All Data Pipelines deployment step exports pipelines without their secrets.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:27.950
The Data Pipelines recipe step imports them as drafts, to publish on the target site.

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:35.355
A step type derives from DataPipelineStepType.

00:08:35.355 --> 00:08:41.580
It names its category, describes the fields it produces, and processes the rows batch by batch.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:47.523
Register it with AddDataPipelineStep, with the display driver of its editor, and it

00:08:47.523 --> 00:08:50.150
appears in the library of the designer.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:57.049
A data source implements IDataSource, or derives from RecordDataSource to declare its fields

00:08:57.049 --> 00:08:58.806
over your own records.

00:08:58.806 --> 00:09:04.580
Once registered, every pipeline, and every module that reads data sources, can use it.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:12.528
Data pipelines turn exports, feeds and transfers into pipelines anyone allowed can design,

00:09:12.528 --> 00:09:14.449
preview, publish and run.

00:09:14.449 --> 00:09:16.060
Thanks for watching.

